2421875, 0.0034389495849609375, -0.0242767333984375, -0.0238189697265625, 0.0274658203125, -0.0003859996795654297, -0.002979278564453125, -0.0016393661499023438, 0.012969970703125, 0.01129150390625, 0.0115966796875, -0.051025390625, -0.004802703857421875, -0.0151824951171875, -0.0040283203125, 0.00200653076171875, 0.00118255615234375, 0.0106048583984375, -0.0211029052734375, 0.0059967041015625, -0.0105743408203125, -0.00959014892578125, -0.0020198822021484375, -0.0188751220703125, -0.038360595703125, -0.0059814453125, 0.019866943359375, -0.0022945404052734375, 0.0135955810546875, 0.0179290771484375, -0.01407623291015625, 0.007755279541015625, -0.0237274169921875, -0.0174102783203125, -0.0304107666015625, 0.0110015869140625, 0.007793426513671875, -0.0290679931640625, 0.00809478759765625, 0.01506805419921875, -0.0134735107421875, 0.02484130859375, 0.0092315673828125, -0.01275634765625, 0.01442718505859375, 0.0038280487060546875, 0.025177001953125, -0.00897979736328125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns is ubiquitous in oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. However, existing methods often struggle to capture transient dynamics or to distinguish between different spatial organizations when global synchronization levels are similar. Identifying and tracking these patterns as they evolve remains a significant challenge, particularly in the presence of complex or short-lived dynamics.\n\nHere, we introduce a framework based on spatial ordinal patterns to characterize the spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems. In this approach, we focus on the phase relationships between neighboring oscillators, rather than their amplitudes. Specifically, for each oscillator, we compare its phase to those of its neighbors and encode the local ordering as a symbolic pattern. To handle cases where phases are nearly equal\u2014situations that standard ordinal patterns may not distinguish\u2014we introduce special symbols to represent ties, ensuring that both phase gradients and synchronized clusters are captured within a single framework.\n\nFrom this symbolic representation, we define a spatial permutation entropy, which quantifies the diversity of spatial ordinal patterns present at each time point. This entropy is computed by evaluating the probability distribution of the observed patterns across the network, providing a measure of the complexity or disorder in the spatial phase arrangement. High entropy indicates a wide variety of local phase configurations, while low entropy reflects more uniform or ordered states. This enables the detection of transient dynamics and regime transitions as they occur.\n\nWe demonstrate that our approach can distinguish between phase-locked states that have identical levels of global synchronization but differ in their spatial organization, such as uniform synchrony versus traveling waves. Additionally, the method effectively characterizes partially synchronized states, where clusters of oscillators are synchronized locally but not globally.\n\nWe validate the method on synthetic oscillator networks, including both regular lattice and random network topologies, across multiple spatiotemporal regimes. Furthermore, we apply the framework to resting-state EEG recordings from human volunteers, where it successfully distinguishes different cognitive or behavioral conditions within individual subjects. These results highlight the potential of our approach for revealing subtle and transient spatiotemporal patterns in both model and real-world oscillatory systems."}}